WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
F3: One of the biggest impediments to getting&nbsp;
things done in the United States Senate when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:12.400
you have a majority but not a 60 seat majority, is&nbsp;
to filibuster. And the filibuster is a mechanism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:20.880
through which even if you only need 50 votes to&nbsp;
pass something, you need 60 votes to end debate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:28.400
on that very issue and move to the votes.
M1: And this is a mechanism that allows a party&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.680
in the minority to indefinitely hold up voting on&nbsp;
legislation and to obstruct and to do all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:42.800
things that Republicans led by Mitch McConnell,&nbsp;
love to do to Democrats. Now, Democrats could blow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.920
up the filibuster completely. This is called the&nbsp;
nuclear option. And we've talked about it before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.120
But it is a double edged sword.
F3: And it could, as I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.640
talked to you before about it, could come back to&nbsp;
really bite Democrats when Republicans are back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:00.800
in the majority at some point in the future. This&nbsp;
has been a point of contention about which there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:06.880
are reasonable positions on both sides. But in an&nbsp;
interview with ABC News that aired last night, Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.840
Biden told George Stephanopoulos he does support&nbsp;
reforming the Senate filibuster, not eliminating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.120
it with the nuclear option, but reforming it.&nbsp;
Well, what exactly is the reform that Joe Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.760
is interested in? Let's take a look at the clip.
M5: So aren't you going to have to choose?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.280
I know you've been reluctant to do away with&nbsp;
the filibuster. Aren't you gonna have to choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.880
between preserving the filibuster and advancing&nbsp;
your agenda? Yes, but here's the choice. I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.480
think you have to eliminate the filibuster.&nbsp;
You have to do it. What it used to be when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:42.160
first got to the Senate and back in the old days&nbsp;
when you used to be around there and that is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.960
a filibuster, you had to stand up and command&nbsp;
the floor and you had to keep talking alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:54.320
You couldn't call for, you know, no one could&nbsp;
say in a quorum call once you stop talking. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.120
lost that. And someone could move in and say,&nbsp;
I moved the question of. So you had to work for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.120
the filibuster. So you're for that reform. You're&nbsp;
for bringing back the talking filibuster. I am.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.840
That's what it was supposed to be. Look, I think&nbsp;
don't hold me to the numbers, George, but I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:14.320
between 1960 and 2000 and I'm making this number&nbsp;
up, I don't know. There are like, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.520
50 filibusters now. They're like two hundred sense&nbsp;
that since just put a hold on it. That's it. Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.760
I mean, you know, so the idea is almost is getting&nbsp;
to the point where there's no democracies having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.440
hard time functioning, a hard time functioning.
M1: Now this is notable for a few reasons. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.120
just a couple of days ago, Joe&nbsp;
Biden's press secretary, Jen Psaki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:40.320
was asked about the filibuster during a press&nbsp;
briefing and she said Joe Biden does not want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.040
to change any rules on the filibuster.
F3: So that seems to have changed. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.840
more importantly, on substance, Biden&nbsp;
doesn't say get rid of the filibuster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.960
just make it more difficult by requiring&nbsp;
an actual talking filibuster. And this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:02.080
is an important detail to understand right now.&nbsp;
Senators can indefinitely extend debate on a topic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.200
and prevent voting on that topic by asking to&nbsp;
extend debate period and just never allowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.400
debate to end. When someone moves to end debate&nbsp;
and vote, Republicans can say, no, I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:20.480
extend debate and they're able to do it. In the&nbsp;
past, senators actually had to hold the floor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:27.360
and speak and keep speaking to delay a bill. And&nbsp;
the idea that Joe Biden is suggesting and others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.680
have also mentioned is to require the actual&nbsp;
speaking to take place so senators can't just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.640
ask for an extension and then another one and&nbsp;
then another one. They actually have to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.600
through the filibuster. And this would have&nbsp;
two effects. First of all, it would require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:49.360
Republicans to actually organize, to have&nbsp;
back to back to back speakers in the Senate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:56.320
never ending with their 50 senators. They actually&nbsp;
would be able to do it. But how long will they be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:03.040
willing to do it from a practical standpoint,&nbsp;
24/7, day after day after day, but maybe more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:10.480
importantly, a talking filibuster that goes on&nbsp;
and on and on merely to prevent a vote on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.320
issue is going to pick up media attention the&nbsp;
longer it goes. And presumably it will come off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.800
as exactly the type of blatant obstruction that&nbsp;
it is. Now, the possible political miscalculation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.680
is that we're assuming the current Republican&nbsp;
Party would be mad about that and they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.680
say this must end, let's just have a vote. But the&nbsp;
current Republican Party has been so propagandized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.200
that they might not actually be that mad about an&nbsp;
endless talking filibuster. They might like it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.720
But the gamble is that the left and the centrists&nbsp;
and maybe some Republicans would say, guys, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.880
we don't have control of the Senate. This isn't&nbsp;
really Democratic. This has to end. You have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.920
put the issue to a vote. Maybe that is what&nbsp;
it what what would happen? I don't know. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:05:00.480
why does this matter? Bigger picture. It matters&nbsp;
because. Filibuster rules in the next two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.960
might actually determine does Washington, D.C.&nbsp;
become a state, does Puerto Rico become a state?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.440
Does automatic voter registration get passed? And&nbsp;
a whole bunch of other important issues? This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:19.440
not trivial. And the point is that right now it's&nbsp;
too easy to filibuster with very little work and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.480
without much political cost. Now, there's a couple&nbsp;
of other ideas that have been floated. One, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:29.600
is not what Biden supports, but these are other&nbsp;
ideas to affect the filibuster without ending it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:38.480
One idea is you have to have 40 senators present&nbsp;
to extend the filibuster. Right now. It's if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:47.360
don't have 60, you can't end debate. The idea is&nbsp;
to flip the onus and put it on Republicans to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:54.640
having 40 of their 50 senators in the chamber, 24&nbsp;
seven to continue extending the filibuster. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.640 --> 00:06:00.560
would be a very good idea without blowing it up.&nbsp;
The other thing is that the talking filibuster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:06.000
it should have to be relevant to the topic at&nbsp;
hand. In other words, if you are trying to end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.560
debate on automatic voter registration and go to a&nbsp;
vote. You can't get up there and read a Dr. Seuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:20.800
book to filibuster, you should be filibustering&nbsp;
materially with something connected to automatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:26.640
voter registration, and that's another possibility&nbsp;
that could be done. We will see. This is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:31.680
big deal. And, you know, it's one of these&nbsp;
things where a lot of this stuff like, oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:38.080
Joe, when is Joe Biden going to do his first press&nbsp;
conference? Sort of like, you know, it's an inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:45.920
the Beltway story that also has very little impact&nbsp;
on Americans. The filibuster is something a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:51.280
people are not following and aren't aware of, but&nbsp;
it actually does relate to things that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:56.640
a very direct and serious impact on Americans&nbsp;
in terms of what it would allow the Senate to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.720
do. So we're going to continue following. Now,&nbsp;
this is this is Joe Biden's latest view on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:07.120
issue. Let's now talk about the flip side of the&nbsp;
coin, which is Mitch McConnell's view on the issue

